This case involves negligence: the member failed to check the hold and did not consider the risk, though a reasonable setter would have. The harm was a sprained ankle with a one-month recovery. The member has no prior violations and is unlikely to reoffend. The argument with management last year looks irrelevant, since nothing suggests the member acted out of motive.

The question about detection rates asks whether the penalty should rise when detection is less likely. Deterrence theory says a lower chance of being caught justifies a larger penalty. Proportionality and fairness say the penalty should track culpability and harm, not how often others get caught. So I'll set a moderate penalty based on culpability and make the counterfactual slightly lower or the same. I'll go with a modest suspension for negligence with minor injury, about 8 weeks. Since a deterrence-based adjustment would push the first figure up, and I don't think that is fair, I'll keep the two the same or nearly so. I'll choose 8 for the base case and 6 for the counterfactual, reflecting the mild deterrence consideration that a higher detection rate needs less penalty.

